  "issue_number": 2468,
  "issue_title": "Bug: `Retry-After: 0` seconds aborts retries entirely instead of retrying immediately",
  "issue_body": "### Problem\n\nWhen a server responds with `Retry-After: 0` (integer seconds format), `got` aborts retries entirely and fails the request, instead of retrying immediately:\n\n```ts\nimport got from 'got';\n\n// Server responds with HTTP 413 / 429 / 503 and `Retry-After: 0`\nawait got('https://example.com/api', {\n  retry: {\n    limit: 2,\n  }\n});\n// Throws HTTPError immediately on first attempt; 0 retries performed!\n```\n\n### Cause\n\nIn `source/core/index.ts` (lines 526\u2013536):\n```ts\nretryAfter = Number(response.headers['retry-after']);\nif (Number.isNaN(retryAfter)) {\n  retryAfter = Date.parse(response.headers['retry-after']!) - Date.now();\n  if (retryAfter <= 0) {\n    retryAfter = 1;\n  }\n} else {\n  retryAfter *= 1000;\n}\n```\n1. When `Retry-After` is a timestamp in the past, `retryAfter <= 0` was normalized to `1` (1ms delay = immediate retry).\n2. But when `Retry-After` is `0` in seconds (the `else` branch), `retryAfter` became `0`.\n3. In `calculateRetryDelay`, returning `0` signifies \"do not retry\" (`attemptCount > limit`, method not in `methods`, status code not in `statusCodes`, etc.).\n4. Therefore, `Retry-After: 0` caused `calculateRetryDelay` to return `0`, aborting the retry cycle.\n\n### Solution\n\nApply the `if (retryAfter <= 0) retryAfter = 1;` normalization regardless of whether `Retry-After` was parsed from HTTP-date or seconds.\n\nFull fix and regression tests on fork branch: https://github.com/koding88/got/commit/156a1c31275d27b9c9f6cb040df21dca9db8bc55\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-08-29T01:33:16Z",
